<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Dōgen</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1200">1200</a>–<a href="/years/1253">1253</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Also known as Dōgen Zenji, Dōgen Kigen, Eihei Dōgen</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Dōgen was a Japanese Zen Buddhist monk, writer and philosopher who founded the Sōtō school of Zen in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>. First ordained in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tendai">Tendai</a> School in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyoto">Kyoto</a>, he grew dissatisfied with its teaching and in 1223 crossed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, where he trained under Rujing of the Caodong lineage and received <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dharma">Dharma</a> transmission in 1227. Back in Japan he promoted zazen, or sitting meditation, and after tension with the Tendai community he moved in 1243 to Echizen province, where his followers built the temple he renamed Eihei-ji in 1246. His writings, above all the Shōbōgenzō, and the Eihei Shingi, the first Japanese Zen monastic code, remain central to Sōtō Zen.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/dogen.jpg" alt="Dōgen" width="330" height="709" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Soto-Zen-Master-Dogen-Zenji-Portrait.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
